st works in the same way  with their host eventually passing away  in the rottnest slowly digesting what  remains and launching spore pods at any  creatures foolish enough to investigate  a truly menacing concept  get out of all the creatures in  subnautica below zero the shadow  leviathan is perhaps the most  intimidating and well known at multiple  points throughout its development  however it looked quite different i'm  curious what was your process like for  designing the shadow leviathan it came  to a fork in the road with that creature  we needed something big and scary  i did a bunch of initial sketches which  had a more traditional almost like a  dragon like head it had glowing white  teeth and huge jaws like a deep sea  viper fish and stuff like that but sort  of vertebrate looking almost but i also  had a bunch of references of  things called arrow worms horrifying  little invertebrates that just have a  face that's basically a bunch of nested  spines coming out of it i just did one  little study where instead of the  dragon-like face it was this more  invertebrate face among all those that  sort of connected with people a little  bit more it was sort of disturbing and  uh combining that with a bit of lamprey  and a few other marine creatures that  people generally find horrifying uh we  moved in that direction it definitely  scared me during my playthrough  and there's one leviathan which didn't  make it into the final game which might  have been even larger than the shadow  leviathan the ice dragon is a cut  creature with a size and design so  intimidating that it lives up to its  mythical namesake we can see from the  concept art that the ice dragon would  swim on wing-like flippers another  visual nod to the dragons of earth myth  this alien dragon wouldn't breathe fire  however but release a stream of brinycle  ice breath to trap vehicles sort of like  a scaled up version of the brine fish in  the final game which alex reese also  designed in fact the